    { n: 10, skill: "Cross-Text Connections", texts: [{ label: "Text 1", body: "Daylight saving time should be abolished. The twice-yearly shift raises heart-attack rates and saves little energy in modern buildings." }, { label: "Text 2", body: "Abolishing the shift is right; locking the clock on permanent daylight saving is not the same as locking it on standard time. Evening light is not free. It is morning darkness moved onto school bus stops. Choose the lock, not only the abolition." }], prompt: "Both authors would most likely agree that", choices: { A: "the twice-yearly clock change is a problem worth ending", B: "heart attacks are unrelated to sleep", C: "school buses should run at midnight", D: "energy savings justify any shift" }, answer: "A", explain: { correct: "Text 1 wants the shift abolished; Text 2 says abolishing the shift is right, then debates which permanent setting.", wrong: { B: "Text 1 cites heart-attack rates.", C: "Text 2 worries about morning bus stops, not midnight buses.", D: "Text 1 says little energy is saved." } } }
