    { n: 10, skill: "Cross-Text Connections", texts: [{ label: "Text 1", body: "Single-stair apartment buildings should be legal again. Extra stairs eat units, and many cities already have old single-stair buildings that have not burned down." }, { label: "Text 2", body: "Old buildings are not a complete fire lab. Smoke behavior, furnishings, and occupant mobility have changed. If single-stair returns, pair it with sprinklers, limited height, and a real inspection culture—not with nostalgia for a floor plan." }], prompt: "The author of Text 2 would most likely", choices: { A: "allow single-stair design only with modern fire mitigations, not as a nostalgia copy", B: "claim extra stairs never eat units", C: "deny that fires happen", D: "argue that inspections are useless" }, answer: "A", explain: { correct: "Text 2 lists sprinklers, height limits, and inspections if single-stair returns.", wrong: { B: "Text 1’s unit-count point is not Text 2’s target.", C: "Fire lab language assumes fires matter.", D: "Inspection culture is required." } } }
